es,\" \"Added styling/Design,\" and \"Added meta tags/SEO\" so the arithmetic stays manageable. I'm working through the DJB2 algorithm character by character for the first string, starting from the initial hash value and multiplying by 33 while adding each character's code, tracking the running total step by step.\n\nContinuing the modular arithmetic computation for h12 and h13, tracking each multiply-add-mod step against 2^32.\n\nContinuing the modular hash computation, I get h18 reducing to 398407916, and now I'm converting that value into hexadecimal by breaking it down against powers of two.\n\nI verify the hex conversion checks out to 398407916, giving me the first hash. Now I'm moving to the second improvement, computing character codes for \"Added headerBranding\" as part of building the next hash value.\n\nContinuing the hash computation...\n\nStill computing the hash values...\n\nSumming the hex digit contributions confirms 0x48a0a145 matches 1218486597, so Hash2 becomes \"HDLmClass48a0a145\".\n\nNow moving to improvement 3, comparing \"Added CTA\" against \"Sales\" concatenated as \"Added CTASales\" (14 chars), I'm listing out each character's ASCII code, and noting the prefix \"Added \" should reuse the same hash value computed earlier in improvement 2.\n\nContinuing the polynomial hash chain, computing each successive term by multiplying by 33 and adding the next character's code, then reducing modulo the appropriate power of two...\n\nContinuing the modular arithmetic, I get h14 = 2922517031, and converting that to hex by repeated division gives 0xae321227, which I'm now double-checking by expanding each digit's place value.\n\nI'm computing hash3 as HDLmClassae321227, then moving to a new comparison: applying the same rolling hash approach to the string \"Added stylingDesign\", starting with the \"Added \" prefix hash and folding in each subsequent character step by step.\n\nStill computing the hash...\n\nContinuing the division to get the hex digits, 